narrate Ashford was the largest thing Corin had ever seen and he hated it within an hour.
narrate Twelve thousand people. Walls, three deep. A market that ran the length of four streets. A temple to the Order with a roof of actual lead, and a clock in the square that told the time, and — this was the thing that finished him — a fountain, in the middle of everything, running clean water day and night, fourteen miles from the eastern line where a woman named Iven had stood in the rain and been told denied.
narrate He stood in front of the fountain for a long time.
corin neutral Don't,
warden neutral I haven't said anything.
corin neutral You've got the face on.
corin neutral It's the same face the boy in Sallow's Rest had. I've read the report.
narrate They gave him a room in the Order's eastern barracks.
narrate It had a window. It had a bed. It had, on the third morning, a girl who came in while he was asleep and changed the water jug, who did not look at him, and who Corin worked out by the fourth day had been told not to look at him.
narrate He was not a prisoner. That was true and it was the worst part of it. The Warden had kept his word on that — no chains, no cell, no interrogation. Corin ate in the hall with the officers. Men nodded to him. A lieutenant asked him, on the second evening, whether he had really stepped over the rope at the eastern line, and when Corin said yes the lieutenant laughed and bought him a drink and told the story to three other men, and all three of them liked him for it.
narrate They liked him for it. That was the thing he could not get past. He had spent eleven weeks learning that the Order was a wall between the Mire and the world, and here was the Order, in a barracks, buying him drinks because he had once refused to obey it.
narrate On the fifth night the Warden came to his room.
narrate He did not knock. He came in, sat down in the chair, put his ledger on the table, and said:
warden neutral Show me the mark.
narrate Corin showed him.
narrate The Warden looked at it for a long time — the circle, the hand inside it, black in the skin of a nineteen-year-old's palm.
corin neutral I have a physician,
corin neutral He would like to cut it out. I have told him that if he tries I will have him transferred to the eastern line.
warden neutral Why?
corin neutral Because I have read everything the Order has written about the deep places in four hundred years,
corin neutral and in all of it there is not one account of a mark being cut out. There are eleven accounts of men who tried. Every one of them ends the same way, and the ending is not a medical event.
corin neutral You will keep your hand.
corin neutral What do you want from me,
warden neutral Eventually? Everything.
warden neutral But not tonight. Tonight I want to know one thing, and then I'll leave you alone.
corin neutral Ask.
warden neutral You stood at that hole in the field with the power to end the Grey Fever. You had it. You still have it.
warden neutral Why did you ask for the Fever and not for her voice?
narrate Corin did not answer for a moment.
narrate He had thought about this. He had thought about it every night since the field, and he had never once got to the bottom of it, and he was not sure he was going to tell this man the truth.
corin neutral You know what he asked for,
warden neutral I know what he asked for.
corin neutral He asked for three days, and it cost him four years, and he's spent twelve years being a monster about it.
corin neutral I asked for the Fever. And I don't know what it's going to cost. And I've decided that I don't get to know first.
narrate The Warden was quiet for a long moment.
corin neutral That,
corin neutral is the single most dangerous sentence I have ever heard a nineteen-year-old say.
narrate He stood up and picked up his ledger.
corin neutral I'll have the eastern line opened,
corin neutral Not all the way. A quota, a thousand a year, medical screening at Ashford. It's not what I promised you and it is more than I promised anyone else in twenty years.
warden neutral Why?
corin neutral Because you asked for the Fever,
corin neutral and I have spent twenty years doing arithmetic, and I have never once met a man who could have asked for the Fever and didn't.
narrate Corin went up onto the wall that night and looked east.
narrate A thousand a year. Out of forty thousand. He did the arithmetic — the thing he had learned from a man he had hit in the reeds and from a man he had drunk with in a barracks — and the arithmetic said forty years.
narrate Forty years to empty the Mire, at a thousand a year, and the Fever would come back inside of eleven.
narrate He looked east for a long time.
narrate He could feel the mark. That was new. In the Mire it had been a scar. Here, on a wall, in a city with a fountain, it had begun to feel like a thing that was waiting.
corin neutral All right,
warden neutral All right.

